}

#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2024-12-28.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="001153"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001153" label="[00:11:53]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: yeah, that's all good, not a rush to merge it</span></div><div class="line" id="001209"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001209" label="[00:12:09]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it can wait into the new year</span></div><div class="line" id="001256"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001256" label="[00:12:56]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i am open to having lines like +++ a/coreutils-5.0/src/cp.c , then we would be applying with -p1, if that's what you mean?</span></div><div class="line" id="030949"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#030949" label="[03:09:49]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fossy: <a rel="nofollow" href="http://files.bootstrapping.world/">http://files.bootstrapping.world/</a>  is now enabled</span></div><div class="line" id="031006"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#031006" label="[03:10:06]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(I'll probably turn on gopher and gemini support later if someone wants it)</span></div><div class="line" id="031123"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#031123" label="[03:11:23]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and 1 torrent magnet per source tarball seems reasonable if one wanted to add a bunch more mirrors easilty (besides people cloning the git repo that mirrors all of the tarballs as well)</span></div><div class="line" id="031213"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#031213" label="[03:12:13]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">we could add a sha256sum written in M0 if we wanted to move validation up to before M2-Planet/mescc-tools-extra</span></div><div class="line" id="031255"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#031255" label="[03:12:55]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">It'll take a couple weeks of effort but it shouldn't be too hard (as we can just do a function by function rewrite in M0)</span></div><div class="line" id="031335"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#031335" label="[03:13:35]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but prior to M0 is a bit of a problem as sha256sum will be *BIG* relative to everything else</span></div><div class="line" id="031345"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#031345" label="[03:13:45]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(at that stage)</span></div><div class="line" id="031425"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#031425" label="[03:14:25]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">CRC however is a viable option in Hex0 if one wanted an early sanity check but it isn't secure</span></div><div class="line" id="032057"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#032057" label="[03:20:57]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aggi: we might end up having to make hardware if we want a reliable long term bootstrap as Intel/AMD plan on getting rid of 32bit kernel mode too</span></div><div class="line" id="032513"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#032513" label="[03:25:13]">&lt;unmush`&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I would be interested in DIY mechanical computers to be used for bootstrapping</span></div><div class="line" id="033419"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#033419" label="[03:34:19]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oriansj: thank you ^-^</span></div><div class="line" id="033427"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#033427" label="[03:34:27]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and they will be so limited in memory and processing power that they won't be able to build gcc nor llvm</span></div><div class="line" id="033451"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#033451" label="[03:34:51]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">sounds like a perfect place to bootstrap plan9, as everything is very minimal there</span></div><div class="line" id="040021"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#040021" label="[04:00:21]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">homo: well plan9 definitely could be a bootstrap path (if anyone cares enough to create it)</span></div><div class="line" id="040254"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#040254" label="[04:02:54]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">unmush`: well there are definitely some pieces one could do in mechanical computers to improve trust (I am partial to paper tape readers but a 4bit CPU definitely could do hex0)</span></div><div class="line" id="050431"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#050431" label="[05:04:31]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">inferno is another alternative bootstrap path, but you'll need 32-bit CPU because it requires 1 MB of RAM :)</span></div><div class="line" id="050515"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#050515" label="[05:05:15]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">maybe that requirement can be reduced, they say pointer size is implementation-specific</span></div><div class="line" id="105251"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#105251" label="[10:52:51]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oriansj: with regards to &quot;free software&quot; the distinction from hardware is superficial anyway</span></div><div class="line" id="105408"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#105408" label="[10:54:08]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">from a purely technical perspective circuit design merely implies a different programming paradigm</span></div><div class="line" id="105454"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#105454" label="[10:54:54]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in this regard &quot;free software&quot; is a misnomer</span></div><div class="line" id="105827"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#105827" label="[10:58:27]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">btw., it wasn't &quot;backwards compatibility&quot; which broke x86, it's the feature creep introduced in parallel to x86_64</span></div><div class="line" id="110124"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#110124" label="[11:01:24]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">meanwhile during christmas i continued sanitizing musl-libc/linux-2.4 ABI/API and hit CLOCK_MONOTONIC</span></div><div class="line" id="110812"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#110812" label="[11:08:12]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">again i've accumulated yet another 300 uncommited files</span></div><div class="line" id="111051"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#111051" label="[11:10:51]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i do keep in mind, some linux-2.x/tinycc distribtion could nicely fit onto some ao486 FPGA type system</span></div><div class="line" id="111245"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#111245" label="[11:12:45]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">linux-2.6.18 probably would be a easier choice than 2.4, but i can't do it all at once, for tinycc support</span></div><div class="line" id="130044"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#130044" label="[13:00:44]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aggi: I believe RMS free hardware design article [<a rel="nofollow" href="https://web.archive.org/web/20150320103808/https://www.wired.com/2015/03/richard-stallman-how-to-make-hardware-designs-free/">https://web.archive.org/web/20150320103808/https://www.wired.com/2015/03/richard-stallman-how-to-make-hardware-designs-free/</a>]  covers it nicely</span></div><div class="line" id="130637"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#130637" label="[13:06:37]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">homo: well if I remember correctly inferno was expected to be made from a plan9 system with a Limbo compiler (which I am not aware of any available under an FSF approved license yet) which someone is going to have to solve the bootstrapping problem for too.</span></div><div class="line" id="171012"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#171012" label="[17:10:12]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Well both Inferno and Plan9 got licensed into MIT so would be surprising for the limbo compiler to not be</span></div><div class="line" id="174724"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#174724" label="[17:47:24]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fossy, oriansj: I need to do more reading, but at first glance IPFS might work better than torrents...</span></div><div class="line" id="174840"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#174840" label="[17:48:40]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">because identical files can be shared across swarms</span></div><div class="line" id="175110"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#175110" label="[17:51:10]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oriansj: yeah, that article about free design has some good points</span></div><div class="line" id="175307"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#175307" label="[17:53:07]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and in general, software developers are not so tied to companies they work for. As a software developer, you can easily come back after work and hack on some FOSS software project, there aren't any huge obstacles there</span></div><div class="line" id="175352"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#175352" label="[17:53:52]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">however, if you work as a hardware designer, you won't be able to come back after work and create hardware chip</span></div><div class="line" id="175518"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#175518" label="[17:55:18]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">you are too tied into the tools and IP that companies use. Also, taping out asic is quite expensive, I guess millions of USD...</span></div><div class="line" id="202812"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#202812" label="[20:28:12]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oriansj I actually re-submitted inferno port to guix <a rel="nofollow" href="https://issues.guix.gnu.org/74734#2">https://issues.guix.gnu.org/74734#2</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="203148"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#203148" label="[20:31:48]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and it builds limbo compiler</span></div><div class="line" id="203307"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#203307" label="[20:33:07]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">limbo compiler is written in C</span></div><div class="line" id="203457"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#203457" label="[20:34:57]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">3 years ago nokia let plan9, plan9port and inferno to be re-licensed from lpl to expat</span></div><div class="line" id="203513"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#203513" label="[20:35:13]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so i was able to get classpath0.93 installed but not without modifying its code somewhat</span></div><div class="line" id="203557"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#203557" label="[20:35:57]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">nothing big: a bunch of makefiles auto-set to -Werror (despite disabling that with configure(?)) and some casting issues</span></div><div class="line" id="203640"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#203640" label="[20:36:40]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but y'alll might want to consider adding your 'configure' line to the instructions -- mine was ./configure --with-jikes --with-gnu-ld --disable-gconf-peer --with-werror=no --disable-plugin</span></div><div class="line" id="203734"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#203734" label="[20:37:34]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">without --disable-gconf-peer classpath asks for gconf, which asks for some corba orbit2 thing which, might be possible to compile but , man it's janky</span></div><div class="line" id="203825"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#203825" label="[20:38:25]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">also kind of curious: gcc has some java functioinality built in nowadays,but this doesn't seem to be leveraged at all, why is this?</span></div><div class="line" id="203855"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#203855" label="[20:38:55]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">is it a matter of 'it's more difficult ot bootstrap from gcc 4.7 to 15 than from jikes to some modern java thing'</span></div><div class="line" id="203858"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#203858" label="[20:38:58]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">?</span></div><div class="line" id="203943"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#203943" label="[20:39:43]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">homo: that was after microsoft bought their mobile division, interesting</span></div><div class="line" id="204246"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#204246" label="[20:42:46]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">tmg1|michelson: gcc java compiler was not suitable for bootstraping anyway</span></div><div class="line" id="204311"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#204311" label="[20:43:11]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fair enoguh</span></div><div class="line" id="204438"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#204438" label="[20:44:38]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I dont remember the location, but there were precompiled jar files in gcj</span></div><div class="line" id="204502"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#204502" label="[20:45:02]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">damn</span></div><div class="line" id="204700"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#204700" label="[20:47:00]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh it was probably ecj.jar</span></div><div class="line" id="204707"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#204707" label="[20:47:07]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(eclipse java compiler)</span></div><div class="line" id="204714"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#204714" label="[20:47:14]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://issues.guix.gnu.org/26966">https://issues.guix.gnu.org/26966</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="204855"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#204855" label="[20:48:55]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and jikes to java bootstrap involves building ecj...</span></div><div class="line" id="204907"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#204907" label="[20:49:07]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">tgm1|michelson microsoft bought mobile division of nokia? well, that explains why those phones now are so shitty they break in less than half a year</span></div><div class="line" id="204911"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#204911" label="[20:49:11]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">once you have ecj, you can quickly build jamvm 2 and use that to build java 7</span></div><div class="line" id="204914"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#204914" label="[20:49:14]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh interesting the configure flags are there</span></div><div class="line" id="204931"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#204931" label="[20:49:31]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">y'all could probably just link to that thread even on your bootstrappable java page</span></div><div class="line" id="205042"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#205042" label="[20:50:42]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">homo: yeah right after i quit my job in like '13 (nokia siemens networks was one of the companies we supported interconnection to) <a rel="nofollow" href="https://www.theregister.com/2024/05/05/microsoft_nokia_anniversary/">https://www.theregister.com/2024/05/05/microsoft_nokia_anniversary/</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="205052"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#205052" label="[20:50:52]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://www.bbc.com/news/business-23940171">https://www.bbc.com/news/business-23940171</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="205223"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#205223" label="[20:52:23]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">jikes was also not easy to find - i've mirrored 1.20 and 1.22 on my mega share <a rel="nofollow" href="https://mega.nz/file/ZhoUHJQS#bAIF7CUf_QpVvcXgkyc-qPcHPlTaUeUNB3AOZARvBnc">https://mega.nz/file/ZhoUHJQS#bAIF7CUf_QpVvcXgkyc-qPcHPlTaUeUNB3AOZARvBnc</a>   <a rel="nofollow" href="https://mega.nz/file/twYQBAiL#ZeArCYQ2lb0dJdSMvnBpLKwhu2NOYp4frnMWuvNBPa4">https://mega.nz/file/twYQBAiL#ZeArCYQ2lb0dJdSMvnBpLKwhu2NOYp4frnMWuvNBPa4</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="205226"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#205226" label="[20:52:26]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">first they destroyed development of plan9, turned nokia phones into shit, and now when it is too late they decided to let go of lpl license</span></div><div class="line" id="205356"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#205356" label="[20:53:56]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">homo: exactly what you'd expect if they are going to implement plan9 stuff into windows</span></div><div class="line" id="205436"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#205436" label="[20:54:36]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">why would they want plan9 stuff into windows when windows is a spyware cashcow?</span></div><div class="line" id="205540"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#205540" label="[20:55:40]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">they profit of unnecessary bloat, which is the opposite of plan9</span></div><div class="line" id="205543"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#205543" label="[20:55:43]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">homo: plan9 stuff+spyware</span></div><div class="line" id="205554"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#205554" label="[20:55:54]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">if it's added it's just more bloat *shrug*</span></div><div class="line" id="205610"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#205610" label="[20:56:10]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">though who knows, maybe their dev team wants less bloat internally or something</span></div><div class="line" id="205620"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#205620" label="[20:56:20]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well one of their 3+ dev teams</span></div><div class="line" id="205657"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#205657" label="[20:56:57]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="http://gg6zxtreajiijztyy5g6bt5o6l3qu32nrg7eulyemlhxwwl6enk6ghad.onion/themusicgod1/classpath">http://gg6zxtreajiijztyy5g6bt5o6l3qu32nrg7eulyemlhxwwl6enk6ghad.onion/themusicgod1/classpath</a>  is where my changes for classpath should be</span></div><div class="line" id="205717"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#205717" label="[20:57:17]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">remember when back in the 90s microsoft wanted to destroy free software?</span></div><div class="line" id="205738"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#205738" label="[20:57:38]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">actually never mind that</span></div><div class="line" id="205750"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#205750" label="[20:57:50]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the point is everything microsoft buys turns into shit</span></div><div class="line" id="205858"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#205858" label="[20:58:58]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">at the very least most of the stuff they buy is already proprietary, so not a big loss when that becomes shittier</span></div><div class="line" id="210127"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#210127" label="[21:01:27]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">no doubt</span></div><div class="line" id="210210"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#210210" label="[21:02:10]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">after all major developers ran away from plan9, we don't see them coming back after it was re-licensed under expat</span></div><div class="line" id="210355"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#210355" label="[21:03:55]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">maybe. though not a 'major developer' perhaps there's @p@fsebugoutzone.org he still uses plan9 i think.</span></div><div class="line" id="210429"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#210429" label="[21:04:29]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&quot;To use Pleroma, please enable JavaScript.&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="210432"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#210432" label="[21:04:32]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">no</span></div><div class="line" id="210452"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#210452" label="[21:04:52]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lolol</span></div><div class="line" id="210505"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#210505" label="[21:05:05]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">you don't need to use javascript to use the fediverse</span></div><div class="line" id="210524"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#210524" label="[21:05:24]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">you can even use local non-browser applications</span></div><div class="line" id="210527"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#210527" label="[21:05:27]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">your fediverse client should be able to look up @p@fsebugoutzone.org without that</span></div><div class="line" id="210625"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#210625" label="[21:06:25]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah. pleroma itself has plugins for support for gopher and ssh access even</span></div><div class="line" id="210654"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#210654" label="[21:06:54]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">course that just pushes the problem into elixir: exchanging the user running javascript for the server running elixir</span></div><div class="line" id="210702"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#210702" label="[21:07:02]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">perhaps not quite so bad but</span></div><div class="line" id="210823"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#210823" label="[21:08:23]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">anyway, most of development happens in 9front and it is doubtful they have interest in joining plan9foundation to develop original plan9</span></div><div class="line" id="210912"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#210912" label="[21:09:12]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's like bsd 4.x devs joining development of original unix</span></div><div class="line" id="211042"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#211042" label="[21:10:42]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">mirroring jamvm 1.5.1 here <a rel="nofollow" href="https://mega.nz/file/99BRmDrb#MAVdtNC12Pm7wsUHDkboE3qrjPFkU027s7KnX5vfe7k">https://mega.nz/file/99BRmDrb#MAVdtNC12Pm7wsUHDkboE3qrjPFkU027s7KnX5vfe7k</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="213044"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#213044" label="[21:30:44]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">looks like jamvm needs tweaking too</span></div><br /></div></body></html>